<p>In the 19th century, the index to the London <em>Times</em> was compiled by Samuel Palmer. Palmer had an unusual way of working, which apparently wasn&#8217;t noticed until 1890, when a mystified reader, Corrie Thompson, <a href="https://www.google.com/books/edition/Notes_and_Queries/aq8RAAAAYAAJ?hl=en&#038;gbpv=1&#038;pg=PA345&#038;printsec=frontcover">wrote to <em>Notes and Queries</em></a> with some examples:</p>
<ul>
<li>An article about a boy sentenced for stealing a twopenny pie was indexed under &#8220;Atrocious Criminal.&#8221;</li>
<li>An article about a floating chapel loosed from its moorings was indexed under &#8220;Disgraceful Act.&#8221;</li>
<li>An article about a storm at Saône-et-Loire was indexed under &#8220;Fatal Storm.&#8221;</li>
<li>An article about a spell of dry weather was indexed under &#8220;Present Dry Season.&#8221;</li>
<li>An article about two women imprisoned for stealing a mare and for shooting at a man were indexed under &#8220;Rather uncommon for Females.&#8221;</li>
</ul>
<p>Apparently Palmer had simply been compiling headlines without reading the articles. This accounts for entries such as &#8220;Extraordinary Escape of Boy at Erith Station, From a Bull Being on the Line&#8221; and &#8220;Extraordinary Marriage Adventure.&#8221; Thompson wrote, &#8220;One would expect to find this system adopted in a &#8216;table of contents,&#8217; but surely it is out of place in an &#8216;index.'&#8221;</p>
<p>One wonderful exception, which Palmer apparently wrote himself:</p>
<p>&#8220;Death of Jane Thomas in her Mother&#8217;s Arms in Holborn at Joy in Seeing her parent at the Stage Door to Receive her.&#8221;</p>
